        <h2>Copying on iPhone, Pasting on a Mac (and Back)</h2>

        <p>If you own both an iPhone and a Mac, you may not need the phone to save anything at all. Apple's Universal Clipboard shares the clipboard between devices signed in to the same Apple Account with Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, and Handoff turned on. Copy an image on the iPhone, switch to the Mac within a short window, and press Cmd+V on PasteDownloadApp. The image arrives as if you had copied it on the Mac, and you can download it from there as PNG, JPEG, or WebP. The reverse also works. It is often faster than AirDropping a file, because nothing needs to be accepted on the other side.</p>

        <p>Universal Clipboard only holds the most recent item, and it expires after a short time, so copy right before you paste. If nothing arrives, check that Handoff is enabled on both devices, since that is the setting most often switched off.</p>

        <VideoLink
            videoId="aqADk5n99h0"
            title="Copy and Paste Between Your Macs and Other Apple Devices"
            note="Shows the Universal Clipboard setup between a Mac, iPhone, and iPad, including the Handoff setting that has to be enabled for a copied image to cross devices."
        />

        <h2>What to Do When Access Clipboard Just Won't Work</h2>

        <p>Worth being direct about a real limitation here rather than suggesting a workaround that does not exist: PasteDownloadApp's paste zone does not currently include a file-picker fallback. Tapping it triggers the same clipboard-read path described above, nothing more, so it is subject to the same iOS and Android restrictions rather than sidestepping them. If Access Clipboard genuinely will not cooperate after a retry, the reliable options are copying the image again immediately before tapping (a stale clipboard grant is a common silent-failure cause), or reloading the page fresh, since a permission state can get stuck after a failed first attempt.</p>

